[[:db/cas 17592186064051 :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\displaystyle \\int 5 \\cdot 9^x +3 e^x  \\, \\textrm d x &= \\displaystyle 5\\int 9^x \\, \\textrm{d}x+ 3 \\displaystyle \\int e^x \\, \\textrm{d}x\\\n&= 5\\cdot \\dfrac{9^x}{\\ln 9} + 3 e^x +C.\n}\n$$" "Apply the sum rule and take constant factors outside the integrals. Then integrate each exponential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int \\left(5\\cdot 9^x+3e^x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=5\\int 9^x\\,\\mathrm{d}x+3\\int e^x\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\\n&=\\frac{5\\cdot 9^x}{\\ln 9}+3e^x+C.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]]
